n='nsec-tls';tls=rfc(8446);early=rfc(8470);ech=rfc(9849);pkix=rfc(5280)
edit(n,'HTTPS, secure SMTP and IMAP, most APIs, and the first flight of every QUIC connection.','HTTPS and configured secure SMTP/IMAP use TLS. QUIC version 1 uses the TLS 1.3 handshake for authentication and key establishment, while QUIC applies its own packet protection.','TLSnotonlyfirstflightofQUICandnotallversionsclaims.',rfc(9001))
edit(n,'TLS gives you both.','Correctly configured TLS can provide both; peer verification and the negotiated authentication mode matter.','TLSwithoutverificationdoesnotauthenticate.')
edit(n,'**Integrity**: any modification is detected and the connection is torn down.','**Integrity**: protected records reject unauthorised changes under the cryptographic assumptions; this does not prevent packet loss, delay or all denial of service.','Notanywiremodificationguaranteedteardown.',tls)
edit(n,'**Authentication**: the client knows the server holds the private key for a certificate naming that host. Optionally, the server authenticates the client too (mTLS, below).','**Authentication**: in the certificate-based mode below, the client validates the server identity and proof of its private key. PSK modes use a different credential. Client certificate authentication is optional (mTLS, below).','PSKmodehasnocertificate.',tls)
edit(n,'TLS 1.2 (2008) accumulated options, and attackers kept finding ways to use the weak ones: CBC padding attacks (POODLE, Lucky Thirteen), RC4 biases, export-grade ciphers (FREAK, Logjam), and RSA key transport, where stealing the server\'s key later decrypts every recorded session. It also needed **two round trips** before data could flow.','The pre-TLS-1.3 ecosystem retained legacy protocol versions, cipher suites and implementations with weaknesses. TLS 1.2 CBC suites were exposed to timing and padding-oracle implementation attacks; original POODLE targeted SSL 3.0. RSA key transport also lacks forward secrecy: later compromise of its decryption key can expose recorded handshakes using that key. A conventional full TLS 1.2 handshake takes two TLS round trips before client application data, excluding TCP setup and False Start.','SeparateSSL3POODLElegacyecosystemfromTLS1.2 andscopeRSA/RTT.',tls)
edit(n,'There are five cipher suites, all **AEAD** (authenticated encryption),','RFC 8446 originally defines five cipher suites, all **AEAD** (authenticated encryption),','Extensibleregistrynotpermanentfive.',tls)
edit(n,'Key exchange is always **ephemeral** Diffie-Hellman, so every session has **forward secrecy**: a server key stolen tomorrow cannot decrypt traffic recorded today.','Certificate-authenticated TLS 1.3 uses ephemeral key establishment. RFC 8446 also permits PSK-only `psk_ke`, which lacks forward secrecy against later PSK compromise; PSK with ephemeral DH can provide it for post-handshake traffic. Early data has separate limitations below. Secure erasure of ephemeral/session secrets is part of the model.','FalsealwaysDHEalwaysFS.',tls)
edit(n,'## The 1-RTT handshake','## The 1-RTT handshake\n\nThis diagram shows a full certificate-authenticated handshake without HelloRetryRequest, client certificates or early data. It excludes TCP setup; implementation scheduling and transport loss can add delay.','Scope diagram optionalmessages.')
edit(n,'(typically X25519, or increasingly a hybrid post-quantum group such as X25519MLKEM768)','(for example, X25519 when supported)','Removeunsupporteddeploymenttrend; hybriddetailsnotrequired.',tls)
edit(n,'**everything after this point is encrypted**','subsequent handshake messages in this flow are encrypted; record headers and optional compatibility ChangeCipherSpec records are exceptions','Falseeverythingciphertext.',tls)
edit(n,'a signature, with the certificate\'s private key, over a hash of the whole handshake so far.','a signature using the certificate\'s private key over a context-bound transcript value (including a fixed prefix, role-specific context string and transcript hash).','ExactCertificateVerifyinputnotbarehash.',tls)
edit(n,'The client sends its own Finished and can send application data immediately. One round trip, compared with two in TLS 1.2.','The client sends its own Finished and can send application data immediately in this successful flow: one TLS round trip versus two for the conventional full TLS 1.2 flow.','ScopeRTT.',tls)
edit(n,'This protocol **ossification** is why QUIC encrypts almost all of its headers.','QUIC reduces exposed protocol details with packet encryption and partial header protection, while fields such as connection IDs and version remain visible.','QUICdoesnotencryptalmostallheaders.',rfc(9001))
edit(n,'a **session ticket**: a pre-shared key (PSK) for next time.','a **session ticket**: an opaque identity associated with a resumption PSK. The client derives that PSK from resumption secrets and the ticket nonce; the ticket is not simply the raw key.','TicketidentitynotPSK.',tls)
edit(n,'The server cannot tell, because it has not yet received anything fresh from the client. Early data also lacks forward secrecy with respect to the ticket key. Only use it for **idempotent** requests such as `GET`.','TLS does not inherently guarantee non-replay of early data. Servers can implement anti-replay state, but applications must account for the remaining risk across instances and retries. Early data lacks forward secrecy against later compromise of its PSK. Permit only requests whose actual semantics tolerate replay; an HTTP method label or idempotence alone is not a complete safety argument.','Timestampfreshnessandserverantireplaypossible;GETnotuniversalreplaysafe.',tls+early)
edit(n,'Browsers and operating systems ship a **root store** of around 150 trusted roots. Roots are kept offline, so day-to-day signing is done by **intermediate CAs**.','Clients use configured **trust anchors**, often from a browser, operating system or application root store. Store membership varies. Root private keys can be kept offline while intermediate CAs perform routine issuance; this is an operational design, not a property of every CA.','Unverifiedrootcount/universalofflineclaim.',pkix)
edit(n,'Here is a real chain, as served by `www.rfc-editor.org` at the time of writing:','The following chain-shaped example illustrates a leaf, intermediate and cross-signed root. It is not a live assertion of what `www.rfc-editor.org` currently serves:','Noverifieddatedlivetlschainclaim.')
edit(n,'The server sends the leaf and intermediates; the client already has the root. Item 2 is a **cross-signature**: a copy of a newer root signed by an older one, so that old devices which only trust GlobalSign can still build a path.','The server sends its leaf and needed intermediates. A client must independently trust a suitable anchor. Item 2 illustrates a **cross-signed certificate** for the same subject/key, with a different issuer and potentially different constraints, enabling an alternate path where supported.','Crosscertnotliteralcopy/policycanreject.',pkix)
edit(n,'Every certificate is within its validity dates.','The leaf and intermediate certificates in the validation path are within their validity dates; trust-anchor handling is a separate client-policy input.','TrustanchoroutsideRFCpath.',pkix)
edit(n,'For browsers, the certificate carries **Certificate Transparency** proofs: it was logged publicly, so a mis-issued certificate for your domain can be spotted.','Applicable browser policies require **Certificate Transparency** evidence for publicly trusted certificates. A signed certificate timestamp is a log\'s promise to include the certificate, not itself an inclusion proof; SCTs need not be embedded in the certificate. Monitoring helps detect misissuance.','SCTpromise≠inclusionproof andpublictrustscope.',['https://github.com/google/certificate-transparency/blob/master/docs/SCTValidation.md'])
edit(n,'`curl`, Python, Java and many mobile clients do not, and fail with "unable to get local issuer certificate".','Other clients may fail with "unable to get local issuer certificate"; fetching and caching depend on the TLS backend, trust store and configuration, not just the application name.','Curlhasmultiplebackendbehaviors.',['https://curl.se/docs/faq.html'])
edit(n,'Both work poorly in practice: OCSP leaks which sites you visit to the CA, and if the responder is unreachable most clients **soft-fail** and accept the certificate anyway, which is precisely when an attacker would block it.','Direct OCSP requests can reveal certificate lookups to the responder. Some clients **soft-fail** when status cannot be retrieved; others use different revocation mechanisms or policies. Blocking a responder can therefore undermine a soft-fail check.','Unsupportedmostallrevocationweakgeneralization.')
edit(n,'but they make **automated renewal** (ACME) mandatory; certificate expiry remains one of the most common causes of outages.','and make reliable renewal and monitoring more demanding. **Automated renewal** (for example ACME) is a practical approach, not a mathematical requirement imposed by the lifetime alone.','Automationnotmandatoryandunverifiedoutageprevalence.')
edit(n,"When written, this printed `TLSv1.3`, `TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384` and `{'commonName': 'rfc-editor.org'}`.","The negotiated version, cipher and certificate subject depend on the live endpoint and local TLS stack; no fixed output is guaranteed.",'Noverifiedhistoricaloutputrecord.')
edit(n,'ECH only helps when many sites share one provider (a CDN), so the outer name says little. It needs encrypted DNS too, or the DNS query leaks the name instead.','ECH hides the inner ClientHello from passive observers, but the practical anonymity set depends on shared configuration, IP addresses and other metadata; a CDN is not a protocol requirement. Plaintext DNS can independently disclose the requested name. Encrypted DNS protects that hop but does not conceal it from the chosen resolver.','CDNnotnecessary-onlyhelpsabsolute andDNSscope.',ech)
edit(n,'Service meshes such as Istio and Linkerd give every workload a short-lived certificate, often with a SPIFFE identity like `spiffe://prod/ns/pay/sa/api`, and enforce mTLS transparently.','Configured service meshes can issue workload certificates and apply mTLS. For example, Istio uses SPIFFE-style identities and separate authentication/authorisation policies; permissive modes and unenrolled workloads require attention.','Notautomaticallyeveryworkload/strictmTLS.',['https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/security/','https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/security/authentication/authn-policy/'])
edit(n,'TLS 1.3 gives confidentiality, integrity and server authentication in one round trip, with forward secrecy always on and the legacy options removed.','The illustrated TLS 1.3 certificate handshake provides confidentiality, integrity and authentication in one TLS round trip. Forward secrecy depends on the negotiated mode and secret handling; PSK-only and early data are important exceptions.','AlignkeytakeawayFS.',tls)
edit(n,'CertificateVerify proves possession of the key and binds it to the handshake; Finished detects any tampering, including downgrades.','CertificateVerify binds proof of the private key to the transcript. Finished authenticates the negotiated transcript, and downgrade sentinels protect relevant cross-version cases.','Finishedalonecannotuniversalpreventforcedfallback.',tls)
edit(n,'0-RTT early data is fast but replayable, so restrict it to idempotent requests.','0-RTT early data is replayable; require application-specific replay-safe semantics and correct intermediary handling.','Idempotencealoneinsufficient.',early)
edit(n,'revocation is weak, so lifetimes are shrinking towards 47 days.','revocation behavior varies, and the public Web PKI lifetime schedule reaches 47 days in 2029.','Noallrevocationweak.')
edit(n,'## Key takeaways','> [!note] Content omitted after review\n> A universal trusted-root count, live endpoint chain/output, certificate-outage frequency and current key-exchange adoption percentages are not asserted: no reproducible dated measurements were available for those claims in this review. The handshake and chain examples are explanatory models.\n\n## Key takeaways','Visiblegapunverifiableempirical/liveclaims.')
